[White "21) French Type III"]
[Black "Julio Granda Zuniga vs. Marc Narciso Dublan"]
[Result "1-0"]

 { Julio Granda Zuniga – Marc Narciso Dublan, Zaragoza 2012 Learning objective: This game shows how White can give up the c-file, cover Black’s entry points (such as c2) and then simply proceed with his standard kingside plans. The first twenty moves are irrelevant to our study. But I include them to show how this pawn structure can arise from many openings, and not just from the French. } 1. Nf3 d6 2. d4 Bg4 3. g3 Bxf3 4. exf3 e6 5. Bg2 c6 6. O-O g6 7. Re1 Nf6 8. c3 Bg7 9. Nd2 O-O 10. Ne4 d5 11. Nc5 b6 12. Nd3 Nbd7 13. Be3 Qc7 14. f4 Ne8 15. f3 Nd6 16. Bf2 c5 17. h4 cxd4 18. cxd4 Rfc8 19. Ne5 Nc4 20. Re2 Ndxe5 21. fxe5 { After an unusual start, we have reached the thematic structure. The reader should begin by assessing the position. Who is better? One of my students said, ”Black’s obviously better, as he dominates the c-file, and has a strong knight on c4.” Then I asked, ”How exactly do you make use of the c-file?” and suddenly he began to hesitate. When I first studied this game I thought White had to fight for equality, but this superficial evaluation was simply wrong. After a long analysis, I concluded Black is already in very serious trouble, as he cannot create serious threats on the queenside. Black’s position looks okay, to say the least, but there aren’t real entry points down the c-file, since the c2-square is protected. In addition, White does not have any weakness on the queenside. Then, why should White be better in this position? White’s advantage is a result of his space, his ability to protect the c2-square and the d4-pawn. In addition, Black’s kingside was weakened by ...g7-g6, which does not mix well with the e6-d5 chain by weakening dark squares and allowing White to break with h4-h5. } Qd7 { To double rooks on the c-file, as there is not really anything better to do. } ( { If } 21... a5 $6 { planning to follow with ...a5-a4-a3 to create some targets in White’s queenside, then } 22. a4 $1 { threatens b2-b3 trapping the knight, and after } Bf8 23. Rc2 ( { but not } 22... Qd7 22. b3 $6 Na3 24. Rea2 Qc3 { with counterplay } ) 
Qb7 24. b3 Na3 25. Rca2 $16 { followed by Rxa3 gaining two pieces for a rook. PH: Not so fast! Here } b5 26. Rxa3 Bxa3 27. Rxa3 bxa4 $15 { is actually completely fine for Black - White's bishops are not active at all. } ) 
 ( { An interesting attempt is to play: } 21... b5 $5 { If White simply continues his play on the kingside with } 22. h5 $6 ( { Instead, it is best for White to first gain partial control of the c-file with } 21... Qd7 21. Rc2 Qd7 23. Rac1 Bf8 24. Bf1 $14 { and only now continue with a pawn storm against Black’s king. } ) 
b4 23. f4 a5 24. Bf1 a4 25. Be1 Bf8 26. Qd3 { then Black can obtain interesting counterplay with: } b3 $5 ( { but not } 26... a3 $2 27. b3 Nb2 28. Qe3 $16 { when Black’s counterplay runs out of fuel } ) 
27. a3 Rab8 28. Rh2 Bxa3 $5 { Sacrificing a piece to create a passed pawn on the b-file, and now } 29. hxg6 fxg6 30. bxa3 b2 31. Rb1 Rb3 { with an unclear position. } ) 
22. h5 $1 { ⩲ This marks the beginning of a good plan; Granda Zuniga understands it will take a long while before Black’s doubled rooks on the c-file can create any serious threats, so he begins a kingside expansion immediately. } ( { If White chooses to advance his pawns in a different order with } 22. f4 $6 { then } h5 $1 { makes it difficult to continue advancing optimally. } ) 
Rc7 ( { Most humans would not like to play } 22... gxh5 $6 { as it weakens the kingside pawn structure permanently. A good continuation would be: } 23. f4 Rc6 ( 23... f5 $6 { only makes matters worse after } 24. exf6 Bxf6 25. Qd3 $16 { followed by Rae1 and Bh3 pressuring the e6-pawn } ) 
24. Qd3 Rac8 25. f5 $1 { ⩲ With the positional threat of f5-f6. The pawn cannot be captured with } exf5 $2 { due to } 26. Bh3 { followed by Bxf5 , when Black’s pawn structure is simply destroyed. } ) 
23. f4 Rac8 { Black has doubled rooks on the c-file, but how will he continue? } 24. Bf1 { The bishop on g2 was rather useless, and this move has two purposes: 1. To clear the second rank for a future transfer Re2-f2 to support g3-g4 and f4-f5. 2. To prepare a future Bf1-d3 to attack the kingside along the b1-h7 diagonal. } Bf8 { Black’s inferiority becomes apparent, as the rooks are doubled on the c-file but there is no clear-cut path to creating threats. } ( { If } 24... Qb5 { simply } 25. Rc2 $5 { ⩲ followed by Bd3 with an excellent position for White. } ) 
25. Be1 { This move is not really about preventing ...Bf8-b4, but rather about clearing the second rank to continue with g3-g4, Re2-f2 and f4-f5. } b5 26. Qd3 { Covering the e3-square to release the rook. } ( { But not } 26. Rf2 $2 Ne3 27. Qd3 Nxf1 28. Rxf1 Rc4 $15 { when Black’s queenside play begins to work. } ) 
Nb6 { Preparing ...Rc7-c1. } ( { By now it is too late to start pushing queenside pawns with } 26... b4 { since White’s kingside attack arrives first, for example: } 27. g4 a5 28. f5 g5 { Hoping to keep lines closed. } 29. Rf2 a4 30. Qf3 $16 { Followed by Bd3 with multiple threats. } ) 
27. b3 $16 { This move prevents Black’s knight from coming into the game again. } Rc1 { Black’s ”invasion” down the c-file will be short lived. } ( { If Black just waited with } 27... a6 { then } 28. g4 $16 { followed by Rf2 and f4-f5 with a strong attack. } ) 
28. Rxc1 Rxc1 29. Bd2 { Simple and effective; the rook on c1 has nothing to do, so it is forced to retreat. } Rc7 ( 29... Ra1 $2 { is bad because of } 30. Bc3 Rc1 31. Qd2 Rb1 32. Qc2 { and the rook is trapped. } ) 
 ( { Similarly, if } 29... Rd1 $2 { then } 30. Qc2 Ra1 31. Bc3 { and wins. } ) 
30. Rf2 { Attacking the b5-pawn and preparing g3-g4 and f4-f5. } a6 ( { If Black plays } 30... Nc8 { White does not even have to bother capturing the b5-pawn, but instead can continue attacking with } 31. g4 Ne7 32. f5 $1 exf5 33. gxf5 Nxf5 34. Bh3 { winning, since the capture Bxf5 will fatally expose Black’s king. } ) 
31. g4 Nc8 32. f5 { Achieving the typical f4-f5 break against Black’s pawn chain. White’s positional and tactical attack is simply irresistible. } exf5 ( 32... Ne7 { doesn’t help due to: } 33. hxg6 hxg6 ( { or } 33... fxg6 34. f6 Nc6 35. f7 Kh8 ( 35... Kg7 36. Qh3 $18 ) 
36. Bg5 $18 { threatening Bf6 } ) 
34. f6 { Setting up a mating net: } Nc6 35. Rh2 Nxd4 36. Qh3 ) 
33. gxf5 Ne7 34. f6 ( 34. Bh3 $5 { was also winning. } ) 
Nc6 ( { PH: If } 34... Nf5 { then } 35. Bh3 { is a deadly pin. } ) 
35. Bh3 Qe8 36. hxg6 hxg6 37. Kf1 ( { Another option was } 37. Bg4 $5 { followed by Rh2 and Qh3 . } ) 
Nd8 ( 37... Bb4 { doesn’t help: } 38. Bxb4 Nxb4 39. Qd2 Qf8 40. e6 $18 { The pawn cannot be taken by } fxe6 $2 { due to } 41. Bxe6 Kh8 42. Rh2 { when mate is coming. } ) 
38. Bg4 { There is no defence against Rh2 and Qh3. } Qc6 39. Rh2 Bg7 40. fxg7 f5 41. Rh8+ Kxg7 42. Qh3 { Final remarks 1. White was able to win this game because he understood that the structure transformation on moves 17 and 21 was favourable to him. The key was his ability to protect c2 and d4. 2. Black was left without a plan because he could not make use of the c-file and because it was not even worthwhile to attack the well-supported d4-pawn. There are no other plans for Black to try in this position. 3. White’s kingside attack rolls on very easily, but Granda Zuniga’s play on moves 22-30 is worthy of praise. White’s forces were ideally synchronized to neutralize Black’s play while preparing the f4-f5 break. } 
 1-0

