 { Magnus Carlsen – Wang Hao, Wijk aan Zee 2011 Learning objective: This game illustrates White’s kingside attack with h2-h4-h5 after a trade on g6, where Black recaptures ...hxg6. } 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Be3 e6 5. Nd2 Nd7 6. Ngf3 Bg6 7. Be2 Ne7 8. Nh4 c5 ( { More common is } 8... Nf5 9. Nxf5 ( { but not } 8... c5 8. Nxg6 $6 Nxe3 10. fxe3 hxg6 11. Nf3 g5 { when Black has good counterplay } ) 
Bxf5 { when Black is likely to keep his bishop on the h7-b1 diagonal with a good position; this is similar to the game Babaev – Burmakin which we study later in this chapter. } ) 
9. c3 Nc6 $6 ( { It was not too late for } 9... Nf5 $5 10. Nxg6 ( { or } 9... Nc6 9. Nxf5 Bxf5 ) 
hxg6 11. Nf3 Nxe3 12. fxe3 g5 $5 { with reasonable counterplay. } ) 
10. Nxg6 hxg6 { This small structural change must not be taken lightly. On the plus side, the capture ...hxg6 gives additional support to f5 while opening the h-file. Both these factors make the f4-f5 break harder to achieve. On the negative side, a black pawn on g6 means White can break on the kingside with h2-h4-h5 to later invade down the h-file. Now that we understand how the structure affects the plans, we proceed with the game. } 11. Nf3 ( { White will not gain much from f4-f5, for example, after } 11. O-O Be7 12. f4 Qb6 13. Qb3 cxd4 14. Qxb6 Nxb6 15. cxd4 O-O 16. g4 Rac8 17. f5 gxf5 18. gxf5 exf5 19. Rxf5 { Black’s kingside is safe, and after } Nb4 $5 { it is White who must fight for equality. } ) 
Rc8 $2 { Black will not be able to secure a safe place for his king, as he can no longer castle long. } ( { As Postny points out, the variation } 11... cxd4 12. cxd4 Qa5 { is nothing special for Black, as after } 13. Kf1 $1 { ⩲ White will proceed with g2-g3, h2-h4 and Kg2 which is similar to the game. } ) 
 ( { An independent try is } 11... Qc7 $5 12. O-O Be7 13. g3 O-O-O 14. Kg2 Kb8 15. Bf4 $14 { when White has a comfortable advantage, though Black is solid for now. } ) 
12. O-O a6 { The plan is ...b7-b5-b4 or ...c5-c4, but this is far too slow. } 13. g3 Be7 14. h4 $1 { ⩲ Launching a kingside attack; Black needs to react quickly. } b5 ( { Black cannot castle safely as after } 14... O-O 15. Kg2 ( { PH: Here } 14... b5 14. Bd3 { followed by Ng5 and h5 is perhaps even more convincing. } ) 
Qb6 16. Rb1 cxd4 17. cxd4 Na5 18. Rh1 Nc4 19. Bf4 $1 { the break h4-h5 yields a very strong attack. For example: } Nxb2 20. Qd2 Ba3 21. h5 { And now a semi-forced line is } gxh5 22. Rxh5 Rfe8 23. Rbh1 Kf8 24. Rh8 Ke7 25. Bg5 f6 26. exf6 gxf6 27. R1h7 Kd8 28. Rxe8 Kxe8 29. Ne5 $1 { with deadly threats. } ) 
 ( { The typical central break } 14... f6 $5 15. exf6 gxf6 { is quite weakening, and after } 16. Re1 Kf7 17. Bf1 { White has a big advantage. Nevertheless, this move manages to divert attention away from the strong h4-h5 break. } ) 
15. a4 $1 { Opening the a-file and making use of the rook on a1. } ( { Instead } 15. Kg2 { , to follow with Rh1 immediately, allows } cxd4 16. cxd4 Nb6 17. b3 Nb4 { when the threat of ...Nc2 diverts some attention away from the kingside, though White is still much better after } 18. Bg5 $5 { . } ) 
Qb6 ( { Or } 15... c4 16. b4 $1 { closing the queenside, since } cxb3 $2 { only benefits White after: } 17. axb5 axb5 18. Qxb3 $16 ) 
16. axb5 axb5 17. Kg2 c4 18. Ng5 Qd8 { Momentarily preventing h4-h5. } ( { White is also much better after } 18... Bxg5 19. Bxg5 b4 20. h5 Nf8 ( { Or } 20... gxh5 21. Bxh5 g6 22. Bg4 { followed by Rh1 } ) 
21. Rh1 gxh5 22. Bxh5 g6 23. Bg4 $16 { followed by an h-file invasion. } ) 
19. Bg4 $1 { ± Threatening 20.Bxe6. } Bxg5 ( { The alternative } 19... Nf8 { is similar to the game after } 20. Qf3 Bxg5 21. Bxg5 Qb6 22. h5 gxh5 23. Bxh5 Qb7 24. Rh1 $16 { threatening 25.Bxf7+ . } ) 
 ( { The careless } 19... b4 $2 { loses to } 20. Bxe6 $1 fxe6 21. Nxe6 Qb6 22. Qg4 Kf7 23. Nf4 { . } ) 
20. Bxg5 Qc7 21. Rh1 Nb6 22. h5 { Opening the h-file decisively. } gxh5 23. Bxh5 Na4 { This is a standard move in Black’s ...c5-c4 plan, pressuring b2, but here Black is losing the race by several moves. } ( { If } 23... Nd8 { , protecting the f7-pawn, then } 24. Bg4 Rg8 25. Qc2 $1 { and the invasion on the h-file is decisive, for example: } Na4 26. Qh7 Kf8 27. Rh4 Nxb2 28. Rah1 b4 29. Qxg8 $1 Kxg8 30. Rh8 ) 
24. Bxf7+ $1 { A decisive piece sacrifice; note how White’s e5-pawn plays an important role in many critical variations. } Kxf7 25. Qf3+ Kg8 ( { If } 25... Kg6 26. Bf6 $1 { Black is getting checkmated due to the threat of Qg4+ and Qxg7 , and } Kf7 ( 26... gxf6 { 27. Qxf6#; or } Rcg8 27. Rxh8 Rxh8 28. Qg4 Kf7 29. Qxg7 Ke8 30. Qxc7 $18 ) 
27. Bd8 { loses the queen. } ) 
26. Rxh8+ Kxh8 27. Rh1+ Kg8 28. Qh5 $1 { A strong quiet move which decides the game due to the threat of 29.Bf6 with a mating net. } Rf8 ( { The king cannot escape with } 28... Kf8 { due to } 29. Qg6 $1 { threatening Rh8 , and after } Kg8 30. Bf6 $1 { there is no defence against 31.Rh7 . For example: } Qf7 31. Rh8 $1 ) 
 ( 28... Nxe5 { loses to } 29. Bf4 $1 Rf8 30. Bxe5 Qe7 31. Qg6 $1 { followed by Rh7 . } ) 
29. Bf6 $1 ( { Black resigns as he is either getting mated or losing his queen. } 29. Bf6 Qc8 30. Qh7 Kf7 31. Qxg7 Ke8 32. Rh7 { Followed by Qg6 and a quick mate, or 29... gxf6 30. Qh8+ Kf7 31. Qh7+ Ke8 32. Qxc7 . } ) 
 ( { PH: If } 29. Bf6 Qd7 { then } 30. Qg6 $1 { threatening Rh7 forces mate. Final remarks 1. The capture 10...hxg6 changed the nature of the position, as the h4-h5 break became stronger than the typical f4-f5 break. 2. Black’s biggest problem was an incorrect positional evaluation. White’s d4-pawn was well protected, and the plan ...c5-c4, ...b7-b5-b4 was far too slow against White’s h4-h5 break. If Black had realized this opposite-flank race was lost, he probably would have played 11...Qc7 in order to castle long. 3. In retrospect, I believe 14...f6 was a reasonable option. It certainly looks terrible as the pawns on e6, f6 and g6 make a very vulnerable team. However, by trading the e5-pawn White’s attacking chances are reduced and Black manages to stay in the game for longer. } ) 

 1-0

