 { Sergei Zhigalko – Julian Radulski, Warsaw 2010 Learning objective: This game illustrates how Black may effectively carry out the ...c5-c4 plan, subsequently castling long and creating a kingside attack. } 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. a3 { To play b2-b4, forcing Black to release the tension on d4. } c4 { Pushing ...c5-c4 is often a double-edged decision. The pawn on c5 was attacking the base of White’s chain, the d4-pawn. Once ...c5-c4 has been played, the new base of the chain is the c3-pawn and in order to attack it Black should play ...b7-b5-b4 together with ...a7-a5. This plan is often stronger than attacking the d4-pawn, but it is also much slower. In fact, it is so slow that if Black were to castle short, then White would probably be able to deliver mate before Black manages to break with ...b5-b4; take the previous game as an example. In this game, Black will ensure the safety of his king by castling long. In such a case, White would like to insist on an attack by playing b2-b3 opening lines, but here this is not easy because he has already played a2-a3. That is essentially the strategic justification of the previous moves and this is why Black did not play ...c5-c4 until White had played a2-a3. Now let’s see how the game continued. } 7. Nbd2 Na5 8. Be2 Bd7 9. O-O ( { Over the last couple of years the move } 9. Rb1 $5 { has become more popular, as White wants to open the b-file to have an attack once Black castles queenside. Recent games have continued with: } Ne7 ( { but } 9. O-O Qc7 $5 { is enough to prevent b2-b4 for the moment, obtaining a position similar to the game } ) 
10. b4 ( { PH: Here the computer doesn't want to castle: } 10. h4 O-O-O 11. h5 h6 { and now just rearrange the pieces: } 12. Nf1 Kb8 13. Bf4 $14 ) 
cxb3 11. Nxb3 { The b-file is open, and now if Black attempts to win an exchange with } Ba4 { then simply: } 12. Nxa5 $1 Qxa5 ( { or } 12. O-O Bxd1 $6 13. Rxb6 axb6 14. Bb5 Kd8 15. Nxb7 Kc7 16. Kxd1 Kxb7 17. Ng5 $14 { as in the game Rapport – Apicella, France 2013 } { _ } ) 
13. Qd2 b5 14. Bd3 a6 15. O-O Rc8 16. Qe2 h6 17. Bd2 { With a fighting position as in the game Duda – Wang Yiye, Al-Ain 2013. } ) 
Ne7 10. Rb1 Qc7 11. Ne1 ( { Opening the b-file with } 11. b4 $2 { no longer works due to: } cxb3 12. Nxb3 Ba4 $17 ) 
 ( { Another possible plan was } 11. Re1 Nc8 12. Nf1 Nb6 13. Bf4 Ba4 14. Qc1 Nb3 15. Qe3 { when Black’s king will be safe on the queenside but White will expand on the kingside anyway. However, the game will not be ”one-sided” as White’s kingside attack may become dangerous to his own king. Overall, chances are approximately equal as in the game Nakamura – Pelletier, Biel 2005. } ) 
O-O-O 12. Bg4 Kb8 { So far both players are following rather standard plans; Black’s king is safe on the queenside, while White is ready to begin a kingside expansion. But now: } 13. Nc2 $6 { This is not the best way to proceed. } ( 13. f4 { was a better alternative, and after } g6 14. Ndf3 Bg7 15. Bh3 { the position is complicated, but chances are approximately balanced. White must expand on the kingside with extreme care, so as not to allow strong counterplay. For example, } Rdg8 16. g4 $2 { would certainly be a premature decision, since now } h5 $1 { gives Black an excellent game. } ) 
Ba4 $1 { A good and rather thematic move. This bishop permanently prevents White from obtaining queenside play, and now it also makes White’s manoeuvres more difficult due to the pin. } 14. Nf3 ( { To illustrate why the pin is annoying, notice that the normal } 14. f4 { can now be met by } h5 $5 15. Bh3 ( { not } 15. Bxh5 $2 Bxc2 16. Qxc2 Rxh5 { and wins } ) 
Nf5 16. Nf3 Be7 { with a good game for Black. } ) 
Nc8 15. Qe2 Nb6 { This knight transfer is very typical of the position; it reinforces the control of the queenside, and may help in a future expansion. The position is critical and White needs to find the right way to react on the kingside. } ( { PH: Given that Ng5 and Bg5 would have been strong tries for White on the next move, here } 15... h6 $1 { would have been a strong prophylactic measure. } ) 
16. h4 $6 { But this is not it, as we shall see. } ( { It was better to play } 16. Ng5 $5 { improving the knight: } h6 17. Nh3 Be7 18. Nf4 $5 ( { or } 18. f4 $5 { with a normal position } ) 
 ) 
 { Now the knight is pretty good on f4, as it makes it difficult for Black to play ...f7-f5 (or ...f7-f6) and helps prepare a slow-paced kingside expansion with g2-g3 and h2-h4-h5. } Ka8 $6 { This is a normal move, but it misses a stronger continuation. } ( { A strong option was: } 16... f5 $1 17. Bh3 ( { Or } 16... Ka8 16. exf6 gxf6 { when Black can soon develop an unpleasant kingside initiative. The e6-pawn is untouchable since } 18. Bxe6 $2 { loses to } Re8 { followed by ...Bd7. } ) 
h6 { Black will continue with ...Be7, ...Rdg8 and ...g5 with an extremely powerful kingside attack. } ) 
17. h5 $2 { White does not realize how many problems he is about to face. In fact, this move makes Black’s reaction even stronger, as the h5-pawn will become a target. } ( { Again } 17. Ng5 { would have prevented Black’s strong kingside reaction with ...f7-f5 and after } h6 18. Nh3 g6 19. Bf4 { the game is approximately balanced. } ) 
f5 $3 { A wonderful move! A double exclamation mark is not an exaggeration: the move is both strong and nearly decisive. This is a reaction the reader should never forget: by using a tactical resource, Black achieves the ...f7-f5 push, after which he dominates the game for the following reasons: 1. He clearly controls the queenside; he has nothing to fear on this flank. 2. He can now begin to expand on the kingside with an eventual ...g7-g5. 3. He can easily create an attack against White’s king. 4. White is inferior on both flanks, hence he cannot create counterplay. } 18. Bh3 ( { It is probably better to take on f6, in order to prevent Black’s kingside expansion with ...g7-g5, but in this case } 18. exf6 gxf6 { does not really help White either, since his position is so weakened: } 19. Re1 ( { not } 19. Bxe6 $2 Re8 $19 { followed by ...Bd7 } ) 
Re8 20. Bh3 Bd6 21. Ne3 Qf7 $17 { Black is about to create a strong attack down the g-file, and the h5-pawn is falling. } ) 
 ( { If } 18. Ng5 fxg4 19. Nxe6 Qd7 20. Nxd8 Qxd8 $17 { the two pieces are far stronger than a rook in this position. } ) 
Qf7 { Attacking the weak h5-pawn. } 19. Ng5 ( { If } 19. h6 gxh6 { Black is simply a pawn up, as the doubled h-pawns are quite useful. For example: } 20. g3 Rg8 21. Kh2 Bxc2 22. Qxc2 Be7 23. Qe2 ( 23. Bxh6 $2 { loses a piece to } Qh5 ) 
Bg5 24. Rg1 Rdf8 $17 { With threats such as ...f5-f4 or ...Nb3. } ) 
 ( { PH: The computer disagrees with the author's evaluation here, and laconically gives } 19. g4 $1 fxg4 20. Bxg4 { with the threat of Ng5, and } Be7 21. Kh2 { White can occupy the g-file next with good play. } ) 
Qg8 20. Bf4 h6 21. Nf3 Qf7 ( { Even stronger was } 21... Bxc2 $1 22. Qxc2 Qf7 { winning the h5-pawn and the game after } 23. g3 Qxh5 24. Bg2 g5 { . } ) 
22. Nfe1 Rg8 23. Kh1 Be7 24. Ne3 $6 { White is struggling to manoeuvre, having little space. } ( { More stubborn was } 24. Bh2 Rdf8 25. f4 { but after } g5 26. hxg6 Qxg6 $17 { White remains in deep trouble. } ) 
Rdf8 { Preparing ...Be8 and ...Qxh5. } ( { The premature } 24... Be8 { is answered by } 25. g4 fxg4 26. Qxg4 { though Black preserves an advantage after: } g5 $1 27. Bh2 Qh7 $5 { ∓ } ) 
25. Bh2 Be8 26. g4 $2 { Making things easier for Black. } ( { The last attempt was } 26. f4 Qxh5 27. g4 $1 { since after } Qxh3 ( { or } 27. g4 Qg6 $5 { ∓ } ) 
 { the queen gets trapped: } 28. Rf3 Qh4 29. N1g2 { But even so Black is better after } fxg4 30. Nxh4 gxf3 31. Nxf3 g5 $17 { with the strong threat of ...Bg6-e4. } ) 
f4 27. N3c2 f3 28. Qd1 g6 $19 { Black opens the kingside decisively. } 29. hxg6 Qxg6 30. Rg1 ( 30. Nxf3 { doesn’t help due to } Rxf3 31. Qxf3 Qxc2 { . } ) 
Ba4 { All of White’s forces are tied up. } 31. g5 { Other choices are no better. } ( { For example: } 31. Rg3 Bh4 32. Rxf3 Bxf2 33. Rxf8 Rxf8 34. Qe2 Bxe1 $19 ) 
hxg5 { The h-file will be fatal for White; he simply does not have sufficient space to reorganize his forces. } 32. Rg3 Rh8 33. Bg4 Rh4 34. Rc1 Qe4 35. Bxf3 Qh7 { White had to resign. Final remarks 1. The ...c5-c4 advance by Black had to be combined with long castling, and works especially well if White is unable to open the b-file with b2-b3. 2. Once Black has castled long, White may still expand on the kingside, but there is a chance this plan could backfire as White’s king can now become exposed. 3. Black’s 17th move ...f5 was an extremely strong blow. If Black manages to combine ...c5-c4 with the ...f7-f5 advance, then he has obtained a significant spatial advantage and White is in serious trouble all over the board. It is no surprise that Black was able to win easily afterwards, as there is little White can do to prevent Black’s active kingside play based on the ...g7-g5 expansion. } 
 0-1

