 { Geetha Narayanan Gopal – Viktor Bologan, Gibraltar 2012 Learning objectives: 1. This game illustrates how Black can challenge White’s centre with the ...f7-f6 break. 2. Note how Black’s bad bishop is actually very strong on the h7-b1 diagonal. } 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nf3 e6 5. Be2 Ne7 6. O-O ( { PH: It's probably not worth the time for White to grab the bishop pair with } 6. Nh4 h6 7. Nxf5 Nxf5 { . Here Black is quickly undermining White with ...c5. } ) 
h6 7. Nbd2 Bg6 8. Nb3 Nd7 9. a4 { To me this approach seems artificial, but it is actually well justified. White intends to gain space on the queenside, and then either proceed to a kingside attack, or play c2-c4, to open the c-file. } Nf5 ( { I could not find any games on my database with } 9... a5 { which intends to prevent a4-a5. Probably the reason is that after } 10. Nh4 Bh7 11. Be3 { Black will not be able to gain much from the usual } c6-c5 { , due to the weakening of the b5-square. } ) 
10. a5 a6 { This move may be necessary sooner or later, in order to prevent a5-a6. } 11. Bd2 { I would not dare to call this move a mistake, though it does not seem to contribute to White’s plans. } ( { More consistent was } 11. c4 $5 Be7 12. cxd5 cxd5 13. Bd3 { and only now begin some kingside action after } O-O 14. g4 Nh4 15. Nxh4 Bxd3 16. Qxd3 Bxh4 17. Bd2 f5 18. h3 Rf7 19. f4 { when White had a tiny plus in Pogonina – Gunina, Russia 2012. } ) 
Be7 ( { PH: Now } 11... c5 { would be answered by } 12. dxc5 Nxc5 13. c4 { when White is opening the position while ahead in development. } ) 
12. g4 Nh4 13. Nxh4 Bxh4 14. f4 { White’s kingside actions are a little too rushed in my opinion, and Bologan finds the way to prove it. } Be4 $1 { A key move. } ( { Less precise was } 14... Bh7 { which is answered by } 15. f5 O-O 16. c4 { when White has a small edge. } ) 
15. Bf3 ( { PH: Here } 15. Be3 Be7 { and only then offering the exchange via the d3-square with } 16. Bd3 { would have been stronger, as Black can't escape the exchange. Play would continue } Bxd3 17. Qxd3 { , with the idea to answer } h5 { with } 18. f5 $1 ) 
Bh7 { As Bologan explains in his analysis, the idea behind ...Bg6-e4-h7 is to force White’s bishop to move to the unfavourable f3-square, blocking his rook and requiring two tempos to arrive at the more desirable d3-square. Note the tremendous difference it makes for Black to have his light-squared bishop on h7, instead of the usual c8-square. This ‘bad’ bishop is actually very powerful when it is outside the pawn chain. } 16. Bg2 O-O 17. f5 { And this is the most critical moment in the game. White has pushed the f4-f5 break without much preparation. A premature break is usually met by undermining the control of White’s centre, but how shall Black do so? } Re8 $1 { This is the best alternative. Instead of fighting against the base, Black will hit the front of the chain since the e5-pawn lacks sufficient support. } ( { Black could also attempt to undermine the centre with } 17... c5 18. Nxc5 Nxc5 19. dxc5 Qc7 { but after } 20. Be3 Qxe5 21. Bd4 Qc7 22. b4 Rad8 23. fxe6 fxe6 24. Qe2 Qd7 25. c3 { White has a tiny plus, due to the blocking of the d5- and e6-pawns. } ) 
18. Kh1 $2 { A routine move, allowing Black to take the lead. } ( { It was necessary to play } 18. Qe2 { but Black has a pleasant choice between } Rc8 { , preparing ...c6-c5 and waiting for the right moment to push ...f7-f6 } ( { or simply } 18. Kh1 f6 $5 19. fxe6 Rxe6 20. c4 Be4 { going into complications. } ) 
 ) 
 ( 18. fxe6 $6 { is weak after } Rxe6 { since after ...f7-f6 Black’s forces are much better prepared for the opening of the position. } ) 
f6 $1 { ∓ This is quite a strong counter-break, unleashing Black’s forces, while White’s pieces are poorly coordinated. } 19. Bc3 ( { If } 19. exf6 Qxf6 20. Qf3 exf5 21. gxf5 { the weak f5-pawn and the e-file are serious problems for White to cope with after: } Re7 22. Bb4 Rf7 23. Bh3 Re8 $17 ) 
 ( 19. fxe6 { was no better: } Rxe6 20. exf6 Rxf6 { White’s pieces are just too poorly prepared to meet Black’s threats. For example: } 21. Bb4 Rxf1 22. Bxf1 Qg5 23. Bd3 Nf6 24. Bxh7 Kxh7 25. Qd3 Ne4 { With an almost decisive advantage. } ) 
fxe5 20. fxe6 ( { Somewhat better was } 20. dxe5 { but after } exf5 21. gxf5 Nxe5 22. Nc5 Bf6 $17 { there is little compensation for the pawn. } ) 
Rxe6 21. dxe5 Nxe5 $17 { The game is essentially decided in Black’s favour, as he is a pawn up and has the safer king. White resigned on move 41. Final remarks 1. By analogy with the game Karjakin – Carlsen from the previous chapter, the reader should note how White’s kingside play is reduced to nothing once Black manages to play the ...f7-f6 break. 2. By analogy with the game Maze – Ni Hua from the previous chapter, Black’s bishop was strong on the h7-b1 diagonal, as it was an obstacle to the f4-f5 break, provided protection to the kingside, and emerged as a powerful attacking piece once the f- and e-pawns came off the board. } 
 *

