14... Be4 $1 { A key move. } ( { Less precise was } 14... Bh7 { which is answered by } 15. f5 O-O 16. c4 { when White has a small edge. } ) 
15. Bf3 Bh7 { As Bologan explains in his analysis, the idea behind ...Bg6-e4-h7 is to force White's bishop to move to the unfavourable f3-square, blocking his rook and requiring two tempo's to arrive at the more desirable d3-square. Note the tremendous difference it makes for Black to have his light-squared bishop on h7, instead of the usual c8-square. This bad bishop is actually very powerful when it is outside the pawn chain. } 
