 { Rashad Babaev – Vladimir Burmakin, Balaguer 2006 Learning objective: The following game is an excellent example of playing the ‘French’ structure with Black (maybe choosing the Caro-Kann is a good start!) . It brings together many of the ideas presented earlier in this chapter, and it is a good way to conclude our study of this structure. My comments are intentionally brief as most of the ideas have been explained earlier. } 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nf3 e6 5. Be2 Nd7 6. O-O h6 7. Nbd2 Bh7 8. c3 Ne7 9. Nb3 $6 { Not the most effective move. } ( { Probably better was } 9. b4 $5 { to keep Black from playing ...c6-c5-c4 as in the game. PH: True, but Black is flexible and can then switch to } a5 { when play might continue } 10. Nb3 axb4 11. cxb4 { , and now Black can eye the a4 and c4 squares with } Nc8 12. Bd2 Ncb6 $14 { . } ) 
Ng6 10. Be3 Be7 11. Ne1 O-O 12. g3 ( { PH: White should have played } 12. f4 $1 { , so that it's more difficult for Black to play ...c5 as the e5-pawn is very firmly defended. } Qc7 13. Bd3 $5 c5 { and now the computer offers the original plan of } 14. Nc1 $1 { , just to keep the central pawn chain intact. } cxd4 15. cxd4 { doesn't help Black, while } c4 15. Bc2 b5 { leaves White well positioned for the advance } 16. f5 { . } ) 
c5 13. h4 ( { If } 13. f4 c4 14. Nd2 b5 { Black follows up as he did in the game, since the break } 15. f5 ( 15. g4 { is met by } f6 $1 { ⩱ with good kingside counterplay } ) 
exf5 16. Rxf5 { has no impact on Black’s solid kingside. } ) 
 { As the reader will note, after ...c5xd4 White could easily protect the base of his pawn chain – the d4-pawn. Meanwhile, the kingside attack is not particularly threatening yet, thus Black decides to play: } c4 $1 ( { But not } 13... cxd4 $2 14. cxd4 $14 { as Black will gain nothing from playing on the c-file, as we saw in the game Granda Zuniga – Narciso Dublan. } ) 
14. Nd2 b5 15. a3 ( { PH: White could still have kept some advantage with } 15. h5 $1 Nh8 16. f4 f6 17. Bg4 $14 { . Obviously Black wouldn't want to push his f-pawn, while after } Qb6 { White can flick in } 18. a4 $1 { at a good moment, as Black can't play ...b4 right now. } ) 
a5 { Black is ready to play ...b5-b4 to attack the c3-pawn, which is now the base of White’s chain. } 16. h5 Nh8 17. f4 f6 $1 { ⩱ As we learned previously, Black neutralizes the kingside expansion with the ...f7-f6 break, which in this case also brings the h8-knight into the game via f7. } 18. exf6 $6 { Turning into a French Type I momentarily. } ( { Better was } 18. Ng2 Nf7 19. Bg4 ( { worse is } 19. g4 $6 fxe5 20. fxe5 Bg5 $17 { when White’s kingside play is completely stuck } ) 
Qb6 20. Nf3 Bd3 $15 { and Black’s position is promising, as he will break with ...b5-b4 to attack the vulnerable c3-pawn. } ) 
 ( { PH: Actually, better was } 18. Bg4 $1 { , similar to the variation given to White's 15th move. } Qb6 19. Ng2 Bd3 20. Re1 Nf7 { Black has achieved a decent position, but after } 21. Nh4 $14 { White still has a small advantage. } ) 
Nxf6 19. g4 Nd7 20. Ndf3 Nf7 21. Ne5 Nfxe5 22. fxe5 { Now Black is back into a much improved French Type III structure as he got rid of his previously useless knight on h8, and can proceed with his plan. } b4 $1 { ∓ } 23. axb4 axb4 { This is a good moment to observe what a wonderful position Black has, for the following reasons: 1. Black has completed his queenside plan with ...b7-b5-b4, and now he has a pleasant choice between ...bxc3 to create a vulnerable c3-pawn, or ...b4-b3 to later attack the even more vulnerable b2-pawn. 2. Black’s ‘bad’ bishop is probably the most powerful bad bishop I have ever seen. 3. White’s king is far more vulnerable than Black’s, and his kingside structure is completely inflexible. } 24. Ng2 Rxf1+ 25. Qxf1 b3 $1 { Now everything comes down to whether Black can win the b2-pawn – and he can. So White’s position is already hopeless. } 26. Rxa8 { Necessary to prevent 26...Ra2. } ( { The try } 26. Nf4 { is met by the standard } Ra2 $1 27. Nxe6 Qa5 28. Rxa2 bxa2 29. Qa1 Bb1 { and White is essentially a queen down. } ) 
Qxa8 27. g5 { A sign of desperation in an already lost position. } ( { A passive defence such as } 27. Qc1 { was doomed after: } Qa2 28. Nf4 Ba3 $1 ) 
 ( 27. Bc1 { is met by } Qa1 28. Nf4 Bg5 29. Kg2 Be4 30. Kh3 Qb1 { when White is completely tied up and his position will soon collapse. } ) 
hxg5 28. Bg4 Nf8 29. Qc1 Qa2 30. Bxg5 Ba3 $1 { -+ The triumph of Black’s strategy; the promotion of the b-pawn is now unstoppable and the rest is easy. } 31. h6 ( { Of course } 31. bxa3 b2 { is the idea. } ) 
Qxb2 32. Qf4 Qb1+ 33. Kh2 Qe4 34. Qg3 Qg6 35. hxg7 Qxg5 36. gxf8=Q+ Kxf8 37. Qf3+ Ke7 38. Bxe6 Qh6+ 39. Bh3 b2 40. Qxd5 b1=Q 41. Qd7+ Kf8 42. Qc8+ Kg7 43. Qxc4 Qf5 { Final remarks This game brought together many of the ideas learned throughout this chapter. Black did not capture with 13...cxd4 as the first game in the chapter showed us the c-file is not useful if the entry points are covered, as was the case here. Later, Black implemented the ...f7-f6 break to gain counterplay along the f-file and to prevent White from further advance, in similar style to Gopal – Bologan. The ...f7-f6 break combined with having a bishop along the h7-b1 diagonal meant Black was able to stop White’s kingside play altogether. This gave him time to pursue the complete chain-advance plan. The advance ...c5-c4 turned the c3-pawn into the base, while the advance ...b4-b3 turned White’s b2-pawn into the base of the chain. The b2-pawn was harder to defend than the c3-pawn, which in turn was harder to defend than the d4-pawn. Since Black had time, he decided to go after the b2-pawn and he succeeded. This game was a beautiful illustration of these concepts. } 
 0-1

